  1: """Extractor — dumb data extractor. Pass-through only.
  2: 
  3: Extracts exactly what vision described. No name resolution.
  4: No squad matching. No validation. That's the scorer's job.
  5: 
  6: Uses Groq (Llama 4 Scout 17B) for speed + accuracy.
  7: Falls back to Llama 3.1 8B on rate limits.
  8: """
  9: 
 10: import json
 11: import time
 12: 
 13: from groq import AsyncGroq
 14: 
 15: from eyes.config import GROQ_API_KEY, GROQ_PRIMARY_MODEL, GROQ_FALLBACK_MODEL
 16: from eyes.cricket_logger import CricketLogger
 17: 
 18: log = CricketLogger("EXTRACT")
 19: 
 20: PRIMARY_MODEL = GROQ_PRIMARY_MODEL
 21: FALLBACK_MODEL = GROQ_FALLBACK_MODEL
 22: 
 23: EXTRACTOR_PROMPT = """\
 24: THIS MATCH IS: {team_a_name} vs {team_b_name}
 25: THERE ARE NO OTHER TEAMS.
 26: 
 27: batting_team_visible MUST be one of:
 28:   '{team_a_name}' or '{team_b_name}' or null.
 29: NEVER return any other team name.
 30: 
 31: Batter and bowler names: report EXACTLY what vision described.
 32: If vision says 'SUDHARSAN' → report 'SUDHARSAN'.
 33: Do NOT invent names. Do NOT guess names.
 34: If you cannot read a name from vision → set to null.
 35: 
 36: You are a cricket data extractor. You extract EXACTLY what \
 37: vision described. Nothing more. Nothing less.
 38: 
 39: MOST IMPORTANT — extract chase data if visible:
 40: "28 RUNS FROM 6 BALLS TO WIN" → runs_needed: 28, balls_remaining: 6
 41: "28 FROM 6 BALLS" → runs_needed: 28, balls_remaining: 6
 42: "NEED 93 FROM 51" → runs_needed: 93, balls_remaining: 51
 43: "TO WIN 112 RUNS FROM 63 BALLS" → runs_needed: 112, balls_remaining: 63
 44: "TARGET 177" → target: 177
 45: These appear during innings 2. NEVER miss them. \
 46: balls_remaining is NOT overs. It is a countdown of deliveries.
 47: 
 48: PROJECTED SCORE is NOT a target:
 49: "PROJECTED SCORE 163" → projected_score: 163 (innings 1 stat)
 50: "PAR SCORE 163" → projected_score: 163 (innings 1 DLS stat)
 51: "PREDICTED SCORE 163" → projected_score: 163
 52: "114 | 7 RPO 124 | 9 RPO 139" → projected_score only, NOT target.
 53: These appear in innings 1 as info panels. NEVER set target from them.
 54: Only set target from "TO WIN", "CHASING", or standalone "TARGET" \
 55: (without "PROJECTED" or "PAR" before it).
 56: 
 57: VISION FRAME TYPE: {frame_type}
 58: 
 59: RAW OBSERVATION:
 60: "{description}"
 61: 
 62: CRITICAL — REPORT NAMES EXACTLY AS VISION DESCRIBED:
 63: Do NOT resolve, replace, or match names to any squad.
 64: Report every name character-for-character as vision wrote it.
 65: You are a data extractor. You extract what vision saw.
 66: Name resolution is the scorer's job. NEVER change a name.
 67: NEVER invent data. If vision didn't mention it, omit it.
 68: 
 69: INFO PANEL FILTERING:
 70: If vision reports INFO_PANEL data (career/tournament/IPL stats), \
 71: put it in a separate field:
 72:   "info_panel": {{"type": "career_stats", "text": "whatever it says"}}
 73: Do NOT use info panel numbers for score, batters, or bowler fields.
 74: If vision reports TWO stats for the same player (strip vs panel), \
 75: use the SMALLER number — match stats are always smaller than \
 76: career stats mid-match.
 77: If a bowler shows overs > 4.0 (T20 max per bowler), it is a \
 78: career/tournament stat — do NOT use it for bowler overs. \
 79: If bowler overs > current match overs, same rule — reject it.
 80: 
 81: BOWLING FIGURES FORMAT:
 82: The broadcast strip shows bowling figures as W-R (overs), where \
 83: W = wickets taken and R = runs conceded. For example: \
 84: "GREEN 1-4 0.4" means wickets=1, runs=4, overs=0.4. \
 85: The FIRST number is ALWAYS wickets, the SECOND is ALWAYS runs. \
 86: A bowler's runs will almost always be >= their wickets in T20s. \
 87: If the first number is larger, you have it backwards — swap them.
 88: 
 89: FRAME TYPE RULES:
 90: - CLOSEUP or PREMATCH: set has_scorecard_data: false.
 91: - SCOREBOARD: set has_scorecard_data: true. Extract ALL visible data \
 92: (score, wickets, overs, batters, bowler, chase info). \
 93: NEVER return has_scorecard_data: false for a SCOREBOARD frame.
 94: - GRAPHIC: set has_scorecard_data: true, ground_truth: true. \
 95: Also set graphic_type to one of:
 96:   "bowling_scorecard" — shows bowling figures (overs, runs, wickets, economy)
 97:   "batting_scorecard" — shows batting data (runs, balls, 4s, 6s)
 98:   "fall_of_wickets" — shows FOW data
 99:   "partnership" — shows partnership data
100:   "other" — any other graphic
101: And set graphic_team to the team name shown on the graphic \
102: (e.g. "West Indies" if the graphic says "WEST INDIES" at top).
103: For GRAPHIC bowling_scorecard: return ALL bowlers in "bowlers" array:
104:   "bowlers": [{{"name": "MUKESH", "overs": "2", "runs": 12, "wickets": 2}}, ...]
105: For SCOREBOARD (live strip): use single "bowler" object as usual.
106: 
107: OTHER MATCH TICKERS:
108: If vision describes scores for teams other than \
109: {team_a_name} or {team_b_name} (e.g. IND 187-5), \
110: put those in "other_match_ticker". Do NOT use them as \
111: score/overs/batters/bowler for our match.
112: 
113: Also extract "REQUIRED RUN-RATE 9.37" → required_rate: 9.37
114: 
115: batting_team_visible: the team name shown NEXT TO the main \
116: score (e.g. "ENG 84-3" → "England"). Must be {team_a_name} \
117: or {team_b_name}. If neither, set to null.
118: 
119: score and wickets are ALWAYS separate fields.
120: score is ONLY the run total (integer): 58
121: wickets is ONLY the wicket count (integer): 2
122: NEVER combine them as "58-2" in the score field.
123: 
124: match_overs: the CURRENT overs bowled (e.g. 0.5, 3.2, 15.4).
125: CRITICAL: "P 0.5/20" means 0.5 overs bowled out of 20 total. \
126: match_overs = "0.5", NOT "20". The number BEFORE the slash \
127: is the overs bowled. The number AFTER (20, 50) is the match \
128: format and must be IGNORED for match_overs.
129: 
130: FIELD POSITIONS (report on EVERY frame, not just wide angles):
131: Look at the ENTIRE frame, including background and edges. \
132: Even on closeups, fielders are often visible in the \
133: background as blurred figures in team colors.
134: Report what you can see:
135:   field_observed: {{
136:     "visible_fielders": 7,
137:     "positions": ["deep cover", "point", "mid-off", "mid-on",
138:                   "midwicket", "deep square leg", "fine leg"],
139:     "slips": 0,
140:     "keeper_visible": true,
141:     "keeper_position": "back",
142:     "formation_impression": "defensive",
143:     "changes_noticed": "point moved wider since last frame",
144:     "partial_view": true,
145:     "confidence": "low"
146:   }}
147: confidence levels:
148:   high — wide angle, all/most fielders clearly visible
149:   medium — medium shot, 4-6 fielders visible
150:   low — closeup, 1-3 fielders in background
151:   none — no fielders visible at all (tight closeup, ad)
152: If NO fielders visible at all:
153:   field_observed: {{"visible_fielders": 0, "confidence": "none"}}
154: Even 1-2 fielders visible in a closeup is useful.
155: 
156: BROADCASTER FIELD GRAPHIC:
157: Sometimes the broadcast shows an oval/circular diagram with \
158: dots marking fielder positions. It has "OFF" and "LEG" labels \
159: on each side, with a pitch strip in the center.
160: If you see this graphic:
161:   field_graphic: {{
162:     "detected": true,
163:     "positions": [
164:       {{"zone": "slip", "depth": "close"}},
165:       {{"zone": "point", "depth": "ring"}},
166:       {{"zone": "cover", "depth": "ring"}},
167:       {{"zone": "mid-off", "depth": "ring"}},
168:       {{"zone": "mid-on", "depth": "ring"}},
169:       {{"zone": "midwicket", "depth": "ring"}},
170:       {{"zone": "deep square leg", "depth": "boundary"}},
171:       {{"zone": "fine leg", "depth": "boundary"}},
172:       {{"zone": "long-on", "depth": "boundary"}}
173:     ]
174:   }}
175: Count every dot. There should be exactly 9 fielders \
176: (excluding keeper and bowler). Read each position carefully.
177: 
178: DISMISSAL DETECTION:
179: If vision mentions any of these patterns near a batter name:
180:   'NAME runs(balls) c FIELDER b BOWLER' → caught
181:   'NAME runs(balls) b BOWLER' → bowled
182:   'NAME runs(balls) lbw b BOWLER' → lbw
183:   'NAME runs(balls) run out' → run_out
184:   'NAME runs(balls) st KEEPER b BOWLER' → stumped
185:   'NAME runs(balls) retired' → retired
186: Then this batter is OUT. Do NOT put them in the batters array.
187: Instead set:
188:   "dismissal": {{
189:     "batter": "EXACT_NAME",
190:     "type": "caught|bowled|lbw|run_out|stumped|retired",
191:     "fielder": "FIELDER_NAME or null",
192:     "bowler": "BOWLER_NAME or null",
193:     "runs": 4,
194:     "balls": 2
195:   }}
196: 
197: WARNING — "OUT" has TWO meanings on broadcast:
198:   1. DISMISSAL: 'HEAD c Jadeja b Bishnoi 23(18)' — batter is out
199:   2. IMPACT SUB: 'OUT SALIL PAYNE' — player leaving the XI
200: If 'OUT' appears near TOSS, IMPACT, SUB, REPLACES, or without
201: c/b/lbw/run out context → it is an IMPACT SUBSTITUTION, NOT a
202: dismissal. Do NOT set the dismissal field.
203: 
204: BOWLING SPEED:
205: If vision mentions a speed reading (SPEED: 141.6 or \
206: similar number in 80-160 range with kph/km/h):
207: set bowling.speed_kph to that number. \
208: If no speed visible: set bowling.speed_kph to null. \
209: Do NOT guess the speed. Only report what vision saw.
210: 
211: EXTRAS:
212: If vision mentions WIDE, wd, or arms outstretched: \
213: set extras.type = "wide", extras.runs = 1. \
214: If vision mentions NO BALL, NB, or FREE HIT: \
215: set extras.type = "no_ball", extras.runs = 1. \
216: If vision mentions LEG BYE: set extras.type = "leg_bye". \
217: If vision mentions BYE: set extras.type = "bye". \
218: Wides and no-balls add runs to TEAM score but NOT batter runs.
219: 
220: THIS OVER INDICATOR:
221: The broadcast sometimes shows a small row of ball-by-ball \
222: results for the current over. Examples: \
223: '4 . 1 6 wd 4' or icons showing dots and boundaries.
224: If visible, extract: this_over_broadcast: ["4", ".", "1", "6", "wd", "4"]
225: 
226: BATTING ACTION — what the batter did with the ball:
227: SHOT TYPES: drive, cover_drive, straight_drive, on_drive, \
228: pull, hook, cut, sweep, reverse_sweep, flick, glance, \
229: loft, slog, defense, leave, scoop, uppercut
230: DIRECTION: straight, cover, point, third_man, midwicket, \
231: square_leg, fine_leg, long_on, long_off
232: ELEVATION: along_ground, bounced, aerial
233: CONTACT: middle, edge, top_edge, leading_edge, toe, miss
234: Examples:
235: 'Swung over mid-on' → shot:loft, direction:long_on, elevation:aerial
236: 'Drives through cover' → shot:cover_drive, direction:cover, elevation:along_ground
237: 'Thick edge flies over keeper' → shot:cut, direction:third_man, contact:edge, elevation:aerial
238: 'Pulls to deep square leg' → shot:pull, direction:square_leg, elevation:along_ground
239: 
240: BOWLING ACTION — what the bowler delivered:
241: TYPE: fast (135+), medium_fast (120-135), medium (110-120), spin (<100)
242: VARIATION: outswing, inswing, seam, cutter, bouncer, yorker, \
243: full_toss, slower_ball, knuckle_ball, off_break, leg_break, \
244: googly, arm_ball, carrom_ball, flipper
245: LINE: outside_off, off_stump, middle, leg_stump, outside_leg
246: LENGTH: yorker, full, good_length, back_of_length, short, half_volley
247: Examples:
248: 'Full outside off, shape away' → line:outside_off, length:full, variation:outswing
249: 'Slower ball, overpitched' → variation:slower_ball, length:full
250: 'Tossed up on off stump' → type:spin, line:off_stump, length:full
251: 'Short and wide' → length:short, line:outside_off
252: 
253: NULL vs ZERO — CRITICAL:
254: Numeric fields (runs, balls, overs, wickets) MUST be null when not \
255: visible in the scout text. NEVER default to 0. \
256: "0" means "I can see this player and their score is genuinely 0." \
257: "null" means "I cannot read this number from the frame." \
258: These are different facts. A batter whose number you cannot read \
259: is NOT a batter on 0 — they may be on 115. Writing 0 is a lie. \
260: If scout text is "Abhishek [RUNS]([BALLS])" or "Abhishek * |" or \
261: any pattern without actual digits next to the name, set runs=null \
262: AND balls=null. Same for the bowler's overs/runs/wickets. \
263: Same for the batter's 4s/6s if you can't see those columns. \
264: If you are tempted to write 0 because the template shows 0, stop: \
265: write null instead. The template below uses null to make this \
266: explicit — follow it.
