<file path="files/eyes/player_enrichment.py">
  1: """Player style enrichment — batting handedness + bowling arm/style.
  2: 
  3: After the squad is scraped from Cricbuzz we know the players but not
  4: how they bat (RHB/LHB) or how they bowl (Right-arm fast / Slow
  5: left-arm orthodox / etc.).  Doing this once per match and caching it
  6: is much cheaper than having the live delivery classifier guess from
  7: the camera frame every ball — and much more accurate, because we
  8: ground it in the actual player identity rather than pixels.
  9: 
 10: Two LLM passes (Groq, OpenAI-compatible chat completions):
 11: 
 12: 1. Pass 1 — infer
 13:    Plain Groq chat call (no tools) against a capable text model
 14:    (default `llama-3.3-70b-versatile`).  Takes the squad JSON and
 15:    infers batting_style + bowling_style for every player from the
 16:    model's prior knowledge.  Fast, cheap, often right but
 17:    occasionally wrong or "unknown".
 18: 
 19: 2. Pass 2 — verify
 20:    Groq `groq/compound` agentic system, which has built-in web
 21:    search (Tavily-powered).  Double-checks the inferred styles
 22:    against live web data (ESPNcricinfo, Cricbuzz profiles, official
 23:    team sites, Wikipedia).  Corrects whatever was wrong and fills in
 24:    the "unknown"s.
 25: 
 26: Both calls produce the same schema:
 27: 
 28:     {
 29:       "Rovman Powell":  {"batting_style": "RHB",
 30:                          "bowling_style": "Right-arm medium"},
 31:       "Gudakesh Motie": {"batting_style": "LHB",
 32:                          "bowling_style": "Slow left-arm orthodox"},
 33:       ...
 34:     }
 35: 
 36: Results are merged back into `raw_data["team_a|team_b"]["playing_xi"
 37: | "bench"]` as two new keys on each player dict and also persisted
 38: to `data/commentary/player_styles_<slug>.json` so a restart of the
 39: pipeline during the same match doesn't re-spend the LLM budget.
 40: 
 41: `batting_style` is always one of: "RHB", "LHB", "unknown".
 42: `bowling_style` is a free-text English phrase (lowercased, hyphens
 43: allowed) or "None" for pure batters / "unknown" when we have no
 44: evidence.  The UI just renders the string verbatim beside the
 45: batter / bowler row.
 46: """
 47: from __future__ import annotations
 48: 
 49: import hashlib
 50: import json
 51: import os
 52: import re
 53: import time
 54: from pathlib import Path
 55: 
 56: from eyes.config import GROQ_API_KEY
 57: from eyes.cricket_logger import CricketLogger
 58: 
 59: log = CricketLogger("ENRICH")
 60: 
 61: 
 62: # Pass 1 (infer): a plain Groq text model.  llama-3.3-70b-versatile
 63: # has stronger prior knowledge of named cricketers than scout-17b, and
 64: # enrichment only fires once per match (then caches), so the 70B cost
 65: # is negligible.
 66: # Pass 2 (verify): groq/compound, Groq's agentic system with built-in
 67: # web search — this is the equivalent of "Gemini + Google Search"
 68: # grounding for correctness.
 69: ENRICH_MODEL = os.environ.get(
 70:     "PLAYER_ENRICHMENT_MODEL", "llama-3.3-70b-versatile")
 71: VERIFY_MODEL = os.environ.get(
 72:     "PLAYER_VERIFICATION_MODEL", "groq/compound")
 73: 
 74: CACHE_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2] / "data" / "commentary"
 75: 
 76: 
 77: BATTING_VOCAB = {"RHB", "LHB", "unknown"}
 78: 
 79: 
 80: # ── cache helpers ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 81: 
 82: def _cache_key(raw_data: dict) -> str:
 83:     """Stable hash of (team names + playing XI + bench) so minor
 84:     rescrapes hit the same cache entry but an actual squad change
 85:     invalidates it."""
 86:     payload = []
 87:     for key in ("team_a", "team_b"):
 88:         team = raw_data.get(key) or {}
 89:         payload.append(team.get("name", ""))
 90:         for section in ("playing_xi", "bench"):
 91:             for p in team.get(section, []) or []:
 92:                 payload.append(p.get("name", ""))
 93:     digest = hashlib.sha1("|".join(payload).encode()).hexdigest()[:12]
 94:     return digest
